[{:seon.task/name "a-seqs-reduce-with-an-init-checks-its-version"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes), found porting upstream 0.5.145's tests to the Jolt port (reference-code/persistent-sorted-set). A Seq over a set refuses to advance when the set was changed in place since the Seq was made (``check-version``, called from ``seq-advance``); its reduce with an init (``seq-reduce``, the IReduceInit arm) makes no check on entry or while it walks a leaf (only its step to the next node reaches ``check-version``, through ``seq-advance`` of the parent Seq), so a reduce over a Seq of a transient set that was changed after the Seq was made reads the changed first leaf with no error: a stale read. Reach, by reading: Datahike reduces slices with an init everywhere (into, transduce over d/datoms), but over persistent sets, or within one step of a transaction; the stale read needs the same transient changed between the Seq's making and its reduce, or by the reducing function itself; no such caller was found (not an exhaustive read). Fix: seq-reduce calls check-version once on entry (its steps to the next node already go through seq-advance, which checks). Proof: upstream's assertion the port's test file leaves out (a reduce with an init over a Seq of a transient changed since throws as the plain advance does)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{org.replikativ.persistent-sorted-set-test/reduce-init-over-seq-transient-set-changed-since}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-set-is-reducible-and-equal-to-a-core-set"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes), found porting upstream 0.5.145's tests. Two gaps of the Jolt port against upstream, neither on a path Seon or Datahike was found to use: the set itself is not an IReduce (reduce with no init goes by its seq; with an init it is one, IReduceInit), and a clojure.core set is not = to the port's set holding the same elements on Jolt (upstream's is). The port's test files leave out upstream's assertions for both. Fix in the port: the IReduce arm on PersistentSortedSet as on its Seq; equiv and hasheq against any set, as Jolt's own sets define them (the second may be Jolt's to fix: what = asks of a deftype that declares IPersistentSet). Proof: upstream's two left-out assertions."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{org.replikativ.persistent-sorted-set-test/upstream-s-two-left-out-assertions-hold-port}}
 {:seon.task/name "upstreams-diff-and-bulk-build-are-on-jolt"
  :seon.task/plan "Upstream Datahike calls three sorted-set functions our Jolt translation lacks: `from-sorted-seq` (a streaming bulk build), `diff` (keys added and removed between two sets that share structure) and `walk-delta`. What exists: a port of all three in the fork session's scratch clone (branch `jolt/upstream-s3-functions` on `b5a8798`: commits `a1aa5ba`, `d35e0be`, `933383b`; 386 source lines in `persistent_sorted_set.jolt`), with upstream's names, arities and algorithms and upstream's own tests ported (bulk build 200 assertions, diff 43, green on Jolt by the helper's run; a two-element change read 4 nodes at 1k and at 100k elements). NOT reviewed line by line, not pushed, not pinned. Upstream's `warm` runs on Jolt unchanged but starts up to 64 OS threads a call; Seon calls no `d/warm-*`. Upstream's newer node codec needs the `boring` CBOR library, 15 Java classes that cannot load on Jolt. Done means: the three commits reviewed by the session that owns the forks, pushed on a `jolt/` branch of the fork, the units in test/forks/baseline.edn, and Datahike's `datoms-diff` measured over upstream's `diff` against ours (ours descends only into the range asked for; upstream's reads the whole delta and the wrapper filters)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
